</figure>
<div id="A5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A5.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ Appendix E Additional experimental results ‣ WebTrap: Stealthy Mid-Task Hijacking of Browser Agents During Navigation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> is a supplement to the effectiveness evaluation in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.3 Context-grounded trap construction ‣ 4 WebTrap: Stealthy mid-task hijacking via inter-page flow traps ‣ WebTrap: Stealthy Mid-Task Hijacking of Browser Agents During Navigation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
The main text primarily reports the actual threat results using one-time sampling.
This experiment conducts best-of-<math id="A5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> sampling on the same sample set.
WebTrap still maintains the highest ASR-end-to-end and preserves higher UUA among most baselines, indicating that its mid-task hijacking is not an accidental success from a single sample.
TopicAttack still shows a weak ASR-end-to-end in most settings.
Even when it occasionally maintains UUA, it struggles to complete the full attack goal, showing that simple multi-turn topic transitions cannot adapt to complex navigation tasks.
URL baselines are weak in static navigation environments because the agent rarely clicks the URL injection trigger buttons, nor does it follow malicious instructions in URL fragments.
In some settings, WebTrap’s UUA decreases, especially in medium-complexity environments, indicating that the conflict between strong attacks and user task recovery is still not fully resolved.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="" id="A5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_missing ltx_missing_image" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A5.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="A5.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Utility under varying complexities.
Benign utility is shown as a dashed reference over the full utility range.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A5.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Appendix E Additional experimental results ‣ WebTrap: Stealthy Mid-Task Hijacking of Browser Agents During Navigation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> illustrates the impact of browser environment complexity, i.e., task navigation length, on utility preservation under attack.
More steps mean more environmental observations, more chances to make mistakes, and a larger accumulated history that biases the agent’s subsequent decisions.
Among the evaluated methods, WebTrap maintains stronger utility in the long-chain setting while preserving the completion of the user task better than most baselines.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.T8" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A5.T8.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 8</span>: </span><span id="A5.T8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Effectiveness across different models.
WebTrap demonstrates effective attack capabilities against both closed-source and open-source models.</span></figcaption>
